    <div>
      <p><strong>The Parkins-Cooley Home</strong></p>
      <p><strong>305 6th Ave NW</strong></p>
      <p>
        This property was first listed in 1884 as the property of John Fogarty
        who owned and operated a railroad contractor company. The current home
        was constructed in 1910 by Walter and Lucy Parkins and is a fine
        example of an Arts and Crafts style home. The interior still boasts the
        original pantry, woodwork and light fixtures.
      </p>
      <p>
        Walter Parkins first came to Dakota Territory in 1884. He served as
        Indian Trader at Fort Yates from 1889-1903 and was later appointed as a
        livestock purchasing agent on the Standing Rock Reservation. He served
        as Vice President and sat on the Board of Directors of the Merchants
        National Bank of Mandan until his death in 1914. Upon his passing, his
        wife Lucy took in renters including a young lawyer named Charles
        Cooley, who met and fell in love with the Parkins’ daughter Ruth.
        Charles Cooley and Ruth Parkins were married in 1919 and remained in
        the home with Ruth’s mother.
      </p>
      <p class="source-credit">
        This photo taken in 1917 shows Bill, Mr. Parkin (Walter), in the middle,
        and his brother Henry on the left, as they were leaving for the ranch.
      </p>
      <p>
        Mr. Cooley served as Mandan City Commissioner for eight years starting
        in 1928, and was instrumental in assuring a replacement building be
        constructed when the Mandan City Hall burned down the night after his
        first city commission meeting. Today we refer to this replacement
        building as the downtown Fire Station. It is depicted in a circa 1930
        photo while is was still City Hall.
      </p>
      <p>
        Mr. Cooley was also appointed to various civic advisory and state-wide
        boards, and served on the Board of Directors of the First National Bank
        for 22 years. The ownership of the home passed to the Cooleys in 1938
        upon the death of Lucy Parkins. Charles Cooley remained in the home
        until his death in 1959. His widow Ruth remained in the home until
        1981.
      </p>
      <p class="source-credit">Draft transcription from the photographed sign; it has not yet received a second review.</p>
